WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.392 --> 00:00:02.844
- What if I told you that there
was one thing that you could

00:00:02.844 --> 00:00:07.558
do in your 1L year that is
scientifically proven to improve

00:00:07.558 --> 00:00:10.675
your grade by at least a third
of a grade in all of your

00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:12.713
law school classes?

00:00:12.713 --> 00:00:15.848
Well, that thing really
exists and we're going to talk

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:18.575
about what it is and how you
can use it to your advantage

00:00:18.575 --> 00:00:20.798
to improve your grades in law school.

00:00:20.798 --> 00:00:23.798
(upbeat harp music)

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.859
Law professors love to write
academic papers, but unlike

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:31.816
other fields, few actually
test their theories

00:00:31.816 --> 00:00:32.916
in the real world.

00:00:32.916 --> 00:00:36.155
But professors Dan Schwarcz
and Dion Farganis at the

00:00:36.155 --> 00:00:39.338
University of Minnesota Law
School, ran a study that

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:43.439
suggests that law students who
get individualized feedback

00:00:43.439 --> 00:00:46.882
from their professors in one
subject are more likely to do

00:00:46.882 --> 00:00:49.389
better in all of their classes.

00:00:49.389 --> 00:00:50.868
Think about that.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:54.517
If you can get feedback on
how to do better in a law

00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:58.006
school final exam, not only
will you do better in that

00:00:58.006 --> 00:01:00.908
class, but you will do
better in all of your

00:01:00.908 --> 00:01:02.921
law school classes.

00:01:02.921 --> 00:01:06.798
What the professors did is
they ran an experiment on 1L

00:01:06.798 --> 00:01:09.010
students in their first year classes.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.189
What they found is absolutely amazing.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:15.103
Their experiment consisted of
a certain section of students

00:01:15.103 --> 00:01:18.869
who were given individualized
feedback on practice tests

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:20.374
versus a controlled group who received

00:01:20.374 --> 00:01:22.442
no instruction whatsoever.

00:01:22.442 --> 00:01:25.633
Controlling for all other
factors like LSAT scores,

00:01:25.633 --> 00:01:29.422
undergrad GPA, et cetera,
the experimental group saw

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:33.680
their grades increase by
one third of a grade in all

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.056
of their classes.

00:01:35.056 --> 00:01:39.952
That is turning a B+ into
an A- or an A into an A+.

00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:43.232
And it was in every single
one of their classes

00:01:43.232 --> 00:01:44.617
on average.

00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:48.686
You can find this study at The
Journal of Legal Education,

00:01:48.686 --> 00:01:51.531
it's called The Impact of
Individualized Feedback on

00:01:51.531 --> 00:01:53.305
Law School Performance.

00:01:53.305 --> 00:01:56.508
This study is amazing, but
it's not terribly surprising

00:01:56.508 --> 00:01:57.575
when you think about it.

00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:00.358
Traditionally, law school
is an entire semester of not

00:02:00.358 --> 00:02:03.679
knowing what the hell you're
doing followed by a test

00:02:03.679 --> 00:02:05.939
that is worth 100% of your grade.

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:09.278
And the worst part is that
professors almost always use

00:02:09.278 --> 00:02:12.765
issue spotting exams, so
while the subject matter may

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:16.061
change, the tactics stay the same.

00:02:16.061 --> 00:02:20.228
And the test doesn't look like
what you talk about in class.

00:02:21.761 --> 00:02:23.683
But look at the bright side, most students

00:02:23.683 --> 00:02:25.604
won't get any feedback.

00:02:25.604 --> 00:02:27.211
Most don't know that there are

00:02:27.211 --> 00:02:28.692
repeatable strategies to acing

00:02:28.692 --> 00:02:30.041
law school exams.

00:02:30.041 --> 00:02:33.497
And most students put off
taking practice exams until the

00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:37.346
very last minute if they take
any practice tests at all.

00:02:37.346 --> 00:02:41.147
But the thing about practice
tests, is that they give

00:02:41.147 --> 00:02:43.255
you the feedback that you need.

00:02:43.255 --> 00:02:46.356
Even if it's not your professor,
him or herself, that is

00:02:46.356 --> 00:02:48.435
giving you the feedback,
you can other students give

00:02:48.435 --> 00:02:51.153
you the feedback, you can
self evaluate and get the

00:02:51.153 --> 00:02:52.615
feedback from yourself.

00:02:52.615 --> 00:02:56.152
You can learn the
strategies of how to take

00:02:56.152 --> 00:02:58.245
law school essay exams.

00:02:58.245 --> 00:03:02.981
And what this study shows, is
that once you take the leap

00:03:02.981 --> 00:03:05.779
and you start taking practice
exams, you will do better

00:03:05.779 --> 00:03:08.605
not only in the class that
you're taking practice exams,

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:11.538
but you will do better
in all of your classes.

00:03:11.538 --> 00:03:13.643
And remember, you're graded
on a curve so you just have

00:03:13.643 --> 00:03:15.810
to get better than everybody else.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.851
Most people don't have the motivation

00:03:18.851 --> 00:03:20.355
to take practice exams.

00:03:20.355 --> 00:03:21.376
They're daunting.

00:03:21.376 --> 00:03:24.337
It seems like it's a waste of
time as opposed to learning

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:27.527
more subsitive law, but
learning subsitive law

00:03:27.527 --> 00:03:28.742
hits a wall.

00:03:28.742 --> 00:03:30.636
You hit diminishing returns.

00:03:30.636 --> 00:03:34.417
Most people don't spend any
time learning how to take

00:03:34.417 --> 00:03:36.005
law school exams.

00:03:36.005 --> 00:03:38.549
And the way that most classes
are taught is completely

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:42.429
antiquated, but you can
turn that to your advantage.

00:03:42.429 --> 00:03:44.872
Be one of the people that
gets the insights and gets

00:03:44.872 --> 00:03:48.581
the feedback with practice
exams, and courses like

00:03:48.581 --> 00:03:50.215
Legal Eagle and others.

00:03:50.215 --> 00:03:54.375
Learning to do well in law
school is a skill, and skills

00:03:54.375 --> 00:03:57.025
can be practiced and they can be learned.

00:03:57.025 --> 00:03:59.072
Or, in other words, it's like a muscle.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:01.788
As long as you workout that
muscle, that muscle will

00:04:01.788 --> 00:04:04.426
get stronger and if you
don't workout that muscle,

00:04:04.426 --> 00:04:06.758
it will atrophy and law school exams

00:04:06.758 --> 00:04:08.726
are exactly the same way.

00:04:08.726 --> 00:04:11.106
You can do better in law school.

00:04:11.106 --> 00:04:12.613
It's science.

